  "response": "**Setting up the calculation (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(−|¬D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**P(positive test):**\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\n**P(D | +) via Bayes' theorem:**\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Result:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly accurate test, because the condition is rare, most positive results are false positives (in this case, only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition).\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
